model {
  extend basalt.toolsGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "A changed browser publication receives a new served version while protocol 44 and previously qualified publication tuples remain frozen. Only exact reviewed Basalt version, recomputed server hash, browser and source tuples may select a framework adapter. A label can have several released byte identities; selection uses both version and server hash, while conflicting evidence for the same bytes refuses. Recompute bytes before compile, format1 or format2 load, declaration, facet and Workflow execution; numeric protocols, dependencies and labels do not establish lineage. The same immutable host registry reaches local, self-host and remote runners. Unknown or conflicting identity, missing or corrupt objects, changed immutable-build admission and protocol disagreement retain the typed provenance refusal before authored side effects. Native Workflow failures preserve the refusal name and complete comparison as a terminal execution failure. The production build exercises real compiled framework bytes through the native Workflow event wait, preserving its caller and resolving current accounts separately in each durable step. Released p39 and p40 grammar stays frozen. Upstream v2 credential-host protocol 42 maps permanently to Basalt 44, preserving Basalt page protocols 42 and 43 with their released adapters and snapshots; the sync inventory records this mapping. The combined current boundary admits underscore host metadata, while all retained account adapters filter metadata after sealing without changing grants or cardinality. New publication must match the reviewed source and module hashes. Existing retained objects and browser assets are never relabelled or overwritten to pass admission. Released .14/p40 and .15/p41 admission uses twice-reproduced immutable release packages, with exact server and browser hashes; real compilation and both retained formats preserve those bytes before native calls. The installed-version restart proof has its own self-host spec, preserving its complete case and target plan while managed Cloud account scenarios retain a dependency tree without runner control. Moving a compatibility scenario preserves its complete assertions and declared targets without leaving runner-control imports in unrelated suites. Explicit compiler acceptance is bound to one reviewed PR preview and exact release identity. Acceptance refuses skipped dependency preparation, an unavailable cache and unexpected cache reuse. Every request uses a fresh preview Cache API namespace while retaining eager dependency preparation, full-graph key construction and real compilation; production reuse and ordinary previews keep their existing behavior. The preview catalog exposes only the retired repository collaboration snapshot required by the fixed acceptance inventory, with deployment-bound head and cold-mode witnesses. The public installer must create and compile all thirty-three reviewed sources in the fixed reviewed order, retain their exact source identities and per-app failures, then remove only this run's new app IDs. An acceptance receipt separately records live model provider inference and preview MCP, synthetic native client model transport and byte-preserved provider auth, exact model-only grant revocation and verified full-stage teardown. The unreleased candidate tuple binds the complete composed app source, public UI exports, scoped navigation, retained selected-account wire contracts and locked dependencies to twice-reproduced pinned server, browser and package bytes; recorded review admits that exact identity and a build never approves itself. Only a completed empty cache lookup establishes a cold request; lookup failure, timeout or any returned response refuses acceptance before compilation. Acceptance failures print the fixed app name, bounded category and relevant expected and observed digests in the job error and summary while raw process output remains private. Installed-origin refusals name each differing property with safe expected and observed fingerprints; preview origins never enter public diagnostics. Additional historical release identities require immutable producer inputs, an exact retained-server comparison and complete package and browser hashes. Unknown retained identities gain no registry entry."
    }
  }
}
